<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Donation of Constantine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Constitutum Constantini</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Donation of Constantine is a forged Roman imperial decree, probably composed in the eighth century, in which the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> appears to grant Pope Sylvester I and his successors authority over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the western half of the empire. Used to support papal claims throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, it was shown to be a forgery by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-valla">Lorenzo Valla</a> in 1440.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/donation-of-constantine.jpg" alt="Donation of Constantine" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown medieval artist in Rome · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sylvester_I_and_Constantine.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, including the oldest, the document is titled Constitutum domini Constantini imperatoris. It was included in the 9th-century Pseudo-Isidorean decretals. Its origin is debated. One suggestion is that an early draft was made shortly after the middle of the 8th century to assist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-stephen-ii">Pope Stephen II</a> in his negotiations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a>, who in 754 received the pope's anointing as king; the lands Pepin then gave the pope became the Papal States.</p><p>Another view holds that it was a move in Roman church politics to raise the Lateran against the rising Vatican, perhaps composed by a Greek monk in a Roman monastery. The German medievalist Johannes Fried distinguishes the Donation from a shorter, earlier forgery, the Constitutum Constantini, of which he thinks it a later expansion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The text is dated 30 March without a year, and names as Constantine's co-consul in his fourth term (315) a Gallicanus who held the office in 317. It has Constantine profess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and grant Pope Sylvester I imperial insignia and privileges and the Lateran Palace. Rome, the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the western provinces are made over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>. Sylvester is also given supremacy over the four principal sees of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><p>It rests on the 5th-century Acts of Sylvester, in which the pope rescues the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> from a dragon and cures the emperor's leprosy through baptism. Because it implied that Sylvester held imperial rank before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> was inaugurated in 330, it also served to reduce the authority of Constantinople.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The earliest known allusion may be a letter of 778 in which Pope Hadrian I urged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> to follow Constantine's example. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-ix">Pope Leo IX</a> was the first pope to invoke the decree directly, in 1054, in a letter to Patriarch Michael I Cerularius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, citing much of it in the belief that it was genuine. In the 11th and 12th centuries it was often cited in the investiture conflicts. Dante regretted the evil born from the donation.</p><p>Doubt had been raised since 1001, when Otto III's chancery denied its authenticity. In the mid-15th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-of-cusa">Nicholas of Cusa</a> and Reginald Pecocke concluded independently that it was a forgery. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-valla">Lorenzo Valla</a> argued from philology that its language could not belong to the 4th century and could most likely be dated to the 8th. Valla's treatise was taken up by Protestant writers such as Ulrich von Hutten and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>. Caesar Baronius admitted the forgery in his Annales Ecclesiastici, published from 1588 to 1607, after which it was almost universally accepted as such.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Donation of Constantine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around this time the forged <b>Donation of Constantine</b>, claiming that the first Christian emperor had granted the pope authority over the West, was produced; Lorenzo Valla proved it a forgery in 1440.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…of Alfonso V of Aragon, then at war with the pope over the Kingdom of Naples, used the history of Latin words and institutions to show that the <b>Donation of Constantine</b>, long cited in support of the popes’ worldly power, could not have been written in Constantine’s time.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
